WEBVTT

00:00:00.591 --> 00:00:21.071
<v Casey>Hey there — today we've got a Tŝilhqot'in forestry venture rewriting how stewardship planning gets done, and a Nova Scotia mine cutting capital costs by fifty-seven percent through sensor-based ore sorting. Plus the Deep Dive: when a small operator runs on old equipment and thin margins, is that resourcefulness, or a safety gap nobody's built support for? Coming right up.

00:00:34.546 --> 00:00:54.882
<v Casey>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Casey. It's Tuesday, September 29th, 2026. Coming to you from the Cariboo, on lands that have been cared for by the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh peoples since time immemorial. Today's theme is Working Lands &amp; Industry.

00:00:55.882 --> 00:01:07.762
<v Riley>And I'm Riley. Forestry, ranching, mining — the industries that actually run this region, and the machinery, the money, and now the provincial election all trying to reshape who benefits from them.

00:01:08.449 --> 00:01:30.233
<v Casey>Quick weather check first. Out at Horsefly Lake this morning it's clear, about 6 degrees, with a high of 12 later and moderate drizzle rolling in through the day — full chance of precipitation, just a millimetre or so. Williams Lake sits around 7 and overcast, Quesnel's clear at 5, 100 Mile House overcast around 5, and Anahim Lake's sitting right at zero under cloud.

00:01:30.629 --> 00:01:34.085
<v Riley>Bundle up if you're out on the highway today. Let's get into it.

00:01:35.485 --> 00:01:47.573
<v Casey>A lot of today's news is close to the ground — a highway investigation, a community remembrance, and a forestry operation rewriting what stewardship planning looks like around here. Let's start with Highway 97.

00:01:48.851 --> 00:02:08.723
<v Riley>Mounties say charges are possible after an accident that closed Highway 97 near 150 Mile House last Friday night. The stretch shut down for hours while investigators worked the scene, and My Cariboo Now reports the investigation is active. No details yet on the vehicles or the number of people involved.

00:02:09.386 --> 00:02:12.202
<v Casey>No timeline on charges, and RCMP haven't named anyone.

00:02:13.353 --> 00:02:32.665
<v Riley>Sticking with the community side of things — the credit union hosted a silent auction over the weekend to support livestock welfare in Williams Lake. The Tribune's photo essay showed volunteers Pheadra Mayette and Azure Nyman feeding two animals named Dirt and Shee, and the proceeds go toward local animal welfare programs.

00:02:33.904 --> 00:02:49.128
<v Casey>Tomorrow, September 30th, Williams Lake holds a community gathering to honour residential school survivors. The Orange Shirt Society organized it, and it lands on the National Day for Truth and Reconciliation — the Tribune says it builds on ceremonies the Society ran last month.

00:02:50.588 --> 00:03:03.156
<v Riley>That gathering is going to sit alongside something harder to report. The Tribune says masked men gathered near a former residential school site, and Chief Rosanne Casimir issued a public safety warning in response.

00:03:04.393 --> 00:03:19.097
<v Casey>No word yet on who they were, what group they represented, or what they intended by gathering there. The nation's response, as reported, was to warn residents and ask people to avoid the area — not to speculate about motive. That's where the story sits as of this morning.

00:03:19.697 --> 00:03:44.073
<v Riley>On a different note entirely — Nazko First Nation opened phase one of a brand-new cultural site at Stump Lake on Sunday. My Cariboo Now's coverage didn't give a lot of detail on what phase two looks like, but it's a project years in the making, and it's the kind of infrastructure that doesn't show up in resource-sector headlines but matters just as much to how a working landscape actually functions.

00:03:45.330 --> 00:04:01.802
<v Casey>That's a decent bridge into something that touches directly on our theme today. Canadian Forest Industries has a piece on Central Chilcotin Rehabilitation — CCR — describing how the company is using community knowledge to guide land stewardship planning across the Cariboo Chilcotin going forward.

00:04:02.935 --> 00:04:20.223
<v Riley>We've mentioned CCR before. It's a Tŝilhqot'in-owned forestry venture, and this piece is specifically about folding local ecological knowledge into harvest and rehabilitation planning from the start, rather than consulting on a plan that's already been drafted by a licensee's foresters.

00:04:21.565 --> 00:04:32.669
<v Casey>Different model entirely from a licensee filing a plan and asking a nation to sign off after the fact. Here the knowledge shapes the plan before it's written. We'll come back to that in the Deep Dive.

00:04:34.014 --> 00:04:45.070
<v Riley>Last close-to-home item — the 100 Mile Free Press ran photos from Friday's Terry Fox Run at Centennial Park in the South Cariboo. Rain didn't stop the kids from finishing the course.

00:04:46.601 --> 00:04:51.401
<v Casey>Now to a story rippling well past our region, one about a shortage nobody quite planned for.

00:04:52.845 --> 00:05:18.293
<v Riley>The Northern Miner reports North America is building rare earth processing plants faster than it's training the engineers to run them. On August 7th, as President Trump hosted mining executives at a roundtable in Washington, representatives of all fourteen accredited U.S. mining schools attended a parallel Department of Energy session trying to figure out where the workforce for these new plants actually comes from.

00:05:19.656 --> 00:05:29.272
<v Casey>Plants standing empty for lack of operators. That's a familiar shape for anyone who's watched a rural hospital sit short-staffed because there's no doctor willing to relocate.

00:05:30.426 --> 00:05:46.314
<v Riley>The parallel holds up. You can fund and build a processing facility in eighteen months if the capital's there. Training a metallurgical or mining engineer takes years, and enrollment in those university programs has been flat or shrinking for a decade across North America.

00:05:47.912 --> 00:06:02.880
<v Casey>Meanwhile Wall Street's own enthusiasm for the adjacent boom is wobbling. The New York Times reported earlier this week that several companies tied to the data center industry have delayed their IPOs, as public backlash grows over how much electricity those facilities burn.

00:06:04.103 --> 00:06:14.415
<v Riley>Different sector, same underlying tension — can the physical build-out outrun the resources, human or electrical, that are supposed to keep it running once the ribbon gets cut?

00:06:15.546 --> 00:06:29.754
<v Casey>The Northern Miner also covered a mining forum in Colorado Springs, where former U.S. sanctions official Edward Fishman told the room that Washington's hostility toward Canada is undermining its own stated goal of breaking China's grip on minerals processing.

00:06:31.212 --> 00:06:38.460
<v Riley>His point, as reported, was that you can't build a minerals alliance with a partner you're simultaneously hitting with tariffs.

00:06:40.349 --> 00:06:54.357
<v Casey>On the equipment side — Caterpillar's new D11 XE, an electric-drive large dozer, got renewed coverage this week from Canadian Mining Journal. It's Caterpillar's first large dozer built this way, promising lower fuel consumption and higher output on-site.

00:06:55.704 --> 00:07:10.008
<v Riley>That's the kind of capital upgrade a major operation can fold into its equipment replacement cycle without blinking. An independent contractor running two dozers on a woodlot license is watching that price tag from a much greater distance.

00:07:11.275 --> 00:07:28.643
<v Casey>We'll get into exactly who can afford which upgrades, and who can't, in the Deep Dive. Canadian Mining Journal also reported that a team called TS-squared won the inaugural AI4SafeMines student competition, a global contest for mine-safety technology run through the International Minerals Innovation Institute.

00:07:29.956 --> 00:07:40.700
<v Riley>Students building safety tools instead of engineers just filling seats at existing operations. Maybe that's one place where the workforce gap starts closing, from the bottom up.

00:07:42.651 --> 00:07:54.571
<v Casey>On the home-garden front, Country Living ran a piece on so-called "multiplier" crops — vegetables you cut and they keep producing — with suggestions for what to plant in October if you've got even a balcony's worth of space.

00:07:56.035 --> 00:08:15.859
<v Riley>And NPR ran a related piece asking whether home-grown produce is actually safer than what you buy at the store, after a record-breaking summer for foodborne illness pushed more people toward growing their own. Short answer from the experts they quoted: it depends on the farming practice, not the size of the operation.

00:08:17.253 --> 00:08:23.365
<v Casey>File that answer away. It's a preview of an argument we're about to have about mines and mills, not just tomatoes.

00:08:24.647 --> 00:08:48.087
<v Riley>Last story, and it's heavier. Fast Company reports British Columbia has sued OpenAI, alleging the company failed to warn police ahead of the mass shooting at Tumbler Ridge Secondary School in February. The lawsuit is part of a broader legal push arguing AI companies should be treated as operators of their deployed tools, not simply as providers of a service somebody happened to use.

00:08:49.208 --> 00:08:56.344
<v Casey>That case is going to shape how every AI company answers for what its systems do, well past this one lawsuit and well past B.C.

00:08:57.704 --> 00:09:08.480
<v Riley>We use tools from that same industry ourselves to help produce this show, for what it's worth — the accountability question applies to outfits our size too, just at a much smaller scale.

00:09:09.844 --> 00:09:14.260
<v Casey>Let's get into what World Habitat Day means for us here, before we head into the Deep Dive.

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:42.828
<v Riley>It's World Habitat Day, and that's a good moment to talk about Habitat for Humanity Cariboo. They build affordable homes for families right here in the region, and their ReStore takes usable building materials — lumber, appliances, fixtures — that would otherwise go to the landfill, and turns the proceeds into funding for those homes. If you've got materials sitting around, or a weekend free, they'll take either. Find them at habitatcariboo.org.

00:09:43.514 --> 00:09:50.730
<v Casey>A ReStore diverting materials out of the landfill is the kind of quiet infrastructure this region runs on and rarely gets credit for.

00:09:51.330 --> 00:09:55.754
<v Riley>Speaking of infrastructure this region runs on — let's get into the Deep Dive.

00:09:57.154 --> 00:10:14.602
<v Riley>Here's the question sitting underneath half of today's stories. When a small operator is surviving on old equipment, thin margins, and local knowledge instead of new capital, is that resourcefulness — or is it a safety and viability gap the bigger licensees simply don't have to own anymore?

00:10:14.943 --> 00:10:21.023
<v Casey>Loaded question, because the answer changes depending on who's asking it and who's paying for the answer being wrong.

00:10:21.684 --> 00:10:45.988
<v Riley>Start with the ore sorting story out of Nova Scotia. EDM Resources ran sensor-based ore sorting tests at their Scotia lead-zinc site and says the technology could cut capital costs there by fifty-seven percent. That's not a small mine's budget shrinking around the edges — that's a small mine's ceiling rising. Sensor-based sorting used to be something only majors could justify deploying.

00:10:46.525 --> 00:10:57.541
<v Casey>Fifty-seven percent on one round of tests, before full-scale deployment. Numbers like that tend to shrink once they leave a pilot phase and meet an actual production schedule with real ore variability.

00:10:58.212 --> 00:11:08.996
<v Riley>Even half of that is a structural shift for a junior operator. A company that previously couldn't afford the processing infrastructure at all now has a plausible path to it.

00:11:09.664 --> 00:11:30.112
<v Casey>Except the rare earth engineer story cuts the other way entirely. North America's building processing plants faster than the workforce that runs them. A junior operation can buy a sorting system off the shelf. Can it hire the metallurgist who actually knows how to calibrate and run it? That's the piece that doesn't scale down along with the equipment cost.

00:11:30.672 --> 00:11:45.088
<v Riley>The majors have the same problem, though. They're competing for graduates out of the same fourteen accredited programs. Nobody's insulated from that shortage — Gibraltar and a two-truck contractor are both bidding into a labour pool that isn't growing.

00:11:45.757 --> 00:11:58.213
<v Casey>Except a major can offer a relocation package and a salary that pulls a graduate away from a small operation entirely. Scale doesn't erase the shortage. It just decides who loses the bidding war for the handful of people who exist.

00:11:58.522 --> 00:12:01.786
<v Riley>No counter to that one. That's the honest version of it.

00:12:02.314 --> 00:12:27.082
<v Casey>Which is where the CCR piece matters more than it looked like at first pass. Central Chilcotin Rehabilitation isn't a lone contractor improvising with duct tape and hoping the season holds. It's Tŝilhqot'in-owned, it has an institutional structure and governance behind it, and it's still relatively small in footprint compared to a major licensee. That's a different animal than a single family operation running two trucks and no safety officer.

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:49.368
<v Riley>Which gets at the actual distinction underneath this whole question. Small isn't one thing. A Nation-owned forestry operation with governance and institutional backing behind it is small and structurally supported. A lone logging contractor with nobody ever checking the equipment is small and structurally exposed. Same word. Opposite reality on the ground.

00:12:49.981 --> 00:12:59.181
<v Casey>And provincial politics doesn't distinguish between those two at all right now. It just talks about volume — tonnes exported, cubic metres cut, gigawatts of LNG capacity.

00:12:59.841 --> 00:13:26.865
<v Riley>That's where the election reporting becomes useful rather than decorative. My Cariboo Now covered both major parties touting resource-sector plans as the campaign entered its second week. Interim Conservative leader Lorne Doerkson promised to double LNG production by 2032 if elected. NDP Leader David Eby talked about growing critical mineral exports substantially, though the piece didn't attach a specific target number to that promise.

00:13:27.483 --> 00:13:38.363
<v Casey>Doubling on one side and vague growth talk on the other. Neither says a word about who's actually pulling the resource out of the ground, what tenure they hold, or what equipment they're running while they do it.

00:13:38.993 --> 00:14:01.657
<v Riley>Doerkson is running again in Cariboo-Chilcotin. He's held that seat since 2020, first elected as a B.C. Liberal, re-elected in 2024 as a Conservative by roughly 7,700 votes. As of the sources we checked, he's the only candidate filed there so far, and nominations remain open for the final stretch of the filing period, so that could still change.

00:14:02.250 --> 00:14:24.410
<v Casey>He's also interim leader of his own party now, since Kerry-Lynne Findlay resigned September 20th following a wave of caucus departures. The Globe and Mail reported the NDP is arguing party bylaws don't actually let an interim leader seek the permanent job — which raises a real, live question about who'd be premier if the Conservatives win this election with Doerkson as leader on the ballot.

00:14:24.981 --> 00:14:36.909
<v Riley>Conservative officials have said they'd hold a two-thirds membership vote to amend the party constitution if it comes to that. That's on the record as a stated plan, not just speculation from critics.

00:14:37.543 --> 00:15:01.943
<v Casey>Prince George-North Cariboo is the same shape, different name. Sheldon Clare is the only filed candidate there too, and he's held that seat since 2024 with a wide margin — about 57 percent of the vote against an independent, an NDP candidate, and a Green candidate combined. He ran for the Conservative leadership himself back in January, lost to Findlay in May, and now watches the interim job go to Doerkson instead of him.

00:15:02.488 --> 00:15:15.416
<v Riley>He said publicly, around September 23rd, that he makes no apologies for backing Findlay, and called her removal "incredibly unfortunate," while also saying he respects the process that put her there in the first place.

00:15:16.079 --> 00:15:34.287
<v Casey>There's an older item that keeps resurfacing every election cycle. Global News reported back in February 2021 that Clare, before he held office, joked about guillotines on a firearms-advocacy podcast responding to federal gun-control legislation. No formal finding or sanction attached to it in anything we've found.

00:15:34.861 --> 00:15:45.277
<v Riley>We haven't found an NDP or Green candidate filed in either riding as of today. That may just be where the nomination window sits right now, with a few days left before it closes.

00:15:45.777 --> 00:16:09.761
<v Casey>Back to the working-lands question, though, because the campaign promises skip right past it. Doubling LNG production or growing mineral exports doesn't say who captures the value when that growth happens. Doubling output at Gibraltar or at a Kitimat-scale LNG terminal is not the same economic event as doubling the number of small operations that supply the trucks, the site prep, the equipment repair around those bigger sites.

00:16:10.440 --> 00:16:37.752
<v Riley>That's a real distinction, and it's exactly where the rural investment piece from Small Farm Canada lands. Canada's investment boom is generating hundreds of billions in commitments across agriculture, energy, and critical minerals. But the framing in that piece was blunt — the commitments exist on paper, and rural and northern communities don't automatically get the infrastructure, housing, or support to actually host that growth once it lands.

00:16:38.293 --> 00:16:52.453
<v Casey>A wave of capital commitments doesn't build a bunkhouse or repave an access road by itself. Somebody still has to decide whose budget line that comes out of, and that decision usually gets made in Victoria or Ottawa, not in the community absorbing the growth.

00:16:53.114 --> 00:17:11.426
<v Riley>Which loops back to the small-operator question directly. If provincial policy is optimizing for headline export numbers, the contractor running two aging trucks isn't a beneficiary of that doubling promise. He's just expected to keep supplying the majors who are the actual beneficiaries, on the same margins he's always had.

00:17:11.972 --> 00:17:25.716
<v Casey>And if his equipment fails on the job site, that failure gets framed as an individual safety lapse — his choice to keep running old gear — rather than a policy failure to fund an inspection regime or an upgrade support tier for operators of that size.

00:17:26.251 --> 00:17:50.579
<v Riley>So the honest split is this. Small can mean the CCR model — institutional backing, Nation ownership, real governance capacity behind the size of the operation. Or small can mean a single rancher or a single logging contractor absorbing every equipment and safety risk alone, because nobody ever built the tier of support that should exist between "major licensee with a compliance department" and "one guy and a truck."

00:17:51.098 --> 00:17:58.602
<v Casey>Same label, opposite exposure. The province's export targets don't ask which one they're talking to when they promise to grow production.

00:17:59.218 --> 00:18:23.714
<v Riley>There's a concrete move buried in that gap, though. The AI4SafeMines competition — students building mine-safety technology, judged through the International Minerals Innovation Institute — is the kind of tool that could scale down to a smaller operator, if anyone built the funding or subsidy path to get it there. Sensor-based safety monitoring doesn't have to stay a major-mine-only line item.

00:18:24.234 --> 00:18:36.818
<v Casey>It doesn't have to be. Whether it will be depends entirely on whether the regulatory side treats small operators as customers worth designing a program for, or as an afterthought once the majors' compliance boxes are checked.

00:18:37.337 --> 00:18:57.505
<v Riley>That's the actual policy question — whether B.C.'s reclamation bonding and safety inspection regime scales its requirements to operator size, the way the ore sorting economics are starting to scale in the other direction, or whether it applies one flat standard that a major absorbs without noticing and a two-truck contractor can't meet at all.

00:18:58.064 --> 00:19:09.064
<v Casey>Nobody we've read has answered that question yet. It's a design choice somebody in Victoria already made, whether they meant to or not, and it's sat there unexamined through at least three governments now.

00:19:09.763 --> 00:19:25.459
<v Riley>Not a resolution, then — an open file, with a specific document somebody with standing could actually go pull. Anyone running equipment out there who knows how those standards land in practice, that's worth hearing about directly — feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:19:26.126 --> 00:19:35.102
<v Casey>If you're operating on the edge of what your gear can safely handle, that gap between "resourceful" and "unsupported" is exactly the story we'd want to chase next.

00:19:35.383 --> 00:19:36.239
<v Riley>Have a good one out there.

00:19:36.419 --> 00:19:37.195
<v Casey>See you tomorrow.